      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Subject: Incident Report - CFM56-7B Engine (#2) Anomaly during Pre-Flight Engine Start\n\nSummary:\n\nDuring the preliminary inspection of Engine #2 on a Boeing 737 utilizing CFM56-7B engines, intermittent fluctuations were observed in the Exhaust Gas Temperature (EGT) and a corresponding vibration within the N2 (Compressor). Diagnostic checks resulted in confirmation of variable EGT readings. Evaluations of both EGT sensors (probes 1 and 2) revealed them free of physical defects. Concurrently, a discrepancy in Turbine Gas Temperature (TGT) was detected, along with minor oscillations in fuel flow identified upon manual review. Immediate action was taken considering the potential risks associated with engine anomaly events.\n\nFollow-up actions include a deeper investigation into the EGT flunctuation and TGT variance, along with systemic analysis to determine the root cause of the observed anomalies. All maintenance personnel are to observe the diagnostic progress, and details of the anomaly will be communicated promptly. The resultant data will contribute to our understanding and remediation of the encountered issue, ensuring the highest safety standards in line with FAA regulations. Further assessments and required corrections will be adequately documented and submitted for FAA review.",
